Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>7  Documents</H1>
<HR>
So far Contacts has had one address book, made up by the program each time it starts. In this chapter it
becomes a proper RISC OS application: its address books are files with a file type and an icon of their
own, you can save them, double-click them to open them, have several open at once, and it asks before
throwing away changes you haven't saved. The program for this chapter is <CODE>!Contacts7</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>A file type of its own</H2>
On RISC OS a file's type decides what happens when you double-click it: Text files open in Edit, Sprite files
in Paint, JSScript files run. An address book should open in Contacts, so Contacts' files need a type of
their own.<P>
A file type is a number from 0 to &amp;FFF. Most are taken - Text is &amp;FFF, Draw files &amp;AFF - and
real applications ask RISC OS Open to allocate them one, so that two programs never use the same number.
For this book we use &amp;1C4, a number no other program on this desktop uses, and call it
<I>Contacts</I>.<P>
Two system variables tell RISC OS about a file type:<P>
<DL>
<DT><CODE>File$Type_1C4</CODE>
<DD>its name, which the Filer shows (in <I>Info</I> and full info displays) and which <I>Set type</I>
understands.<P>
<DT><CODE>Alias$@RunType_1C4</CODE>
<DD>the command that runs when a file of that type is double-clicked, with the file's name added to the end.
<P>
</DL>
They are set in the application's <CODE>!Boot</CODE> file, an Obey file which the Filer runs the first time
it shows the application in a directory display - so as soon as you have seen !Contacts7, its files know
where to go:<P>
<PRE>
| !Boot file for !Contacts7: the Filer runs it when it first shows the
| application, so the icons and the file type are known.
Set Contacts7$Dir &lt;Obey$Dir&gt;
IconSprites &lt;Obey$Dir&gt;.!Sprites
Set File$Type_1C4 Contacts
| double-clicking a Contacts file runs Contacts with it (%% is a %:
| Obey would put its own parameters in place of a plain %*0)
Set Alias$@RunType_1C4 Run &lt;Contacts7$Dir&gt;.!Run %%*0
</PRE>
<CODE>IconSprites</CODE> loads the application's sprites, which now include <CODE>file_1c4</CODE> and
<CODE>small_1c4</CODE>: the Filer shows files of type &amp;1C4 with the sprite called <CODE>file_</CODE>
and the type's number. The last line makes double-clicking a Contacts file run
<CODE>!Contacts7.!Run</CODE> with the file's name after it.<P>
The <CODE>%%</CODE> needs explaining. In an Obey file, <CODE>%0</CODE> to <CODE>%9</CODE> and
<CODE>%*0</CODE> stand for the <I>parameters</I> the Obey file itself was run with, and are replaced before
each line is carried out. We don't want that here: the alias should contain the characters
<CODE>%*0</CODE>, to be replaced later by the name of the file being double-clicked. <CODE>%%</CODE> in an
Obey file stands for one <CODE>%</CODE>, so <CODE>%%*0</CODE> becomes <CODE>%*0</CODE> when the line runs.
<CODE>!Run</CODE> uses a plain <CODE>%*0</CODE>, because there it does mean its own parameters - the file
being opened - which it passes on to <CODE>!RunImage</CODE>. It also names the file type, in case the
application is run before the Filer has shown it (and so before <CODE>!Boot</CODE> has run):<P>
<PRE>
| !Run file for !Contacts7
Set Contacts7$Dir &lt;Obey$Dir&gt;
IconSprites &lt;Contacts7$Dir&gt;.!Sprites
Set File$Type_1C4 Contacts
Run &lt;Contacts7$Dir&gt;.!RunImage %*0
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Contacts" WIDTH=989 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
Here the finished !Contacts has been seen by the Filer: the address book <CODE>Friends</CODE> in
<CODE>HardDisc4.$.Examples.JSApps</CODE> has Contacts' file icon, and double-clicking it opens it.<P>
<B>Note</B>: the directory <CODE>HardDisc4.$.Examples.JSApps</CODE> holds several versions of Contacts - one for each
chapter - and each of the later ones claims &amp;1C4 in its <CODE>!Boot</CODE>. Whichever the Filer showed
last is the one a double-click starts. To be sure which one opens a file, start it first and drag the file
onto its icon on the icon bar.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>An address book as text</H2>
A file holds text (or bytes). As Doodle did in the first book, Contacts turns its data into text with JSON. The
<CODE>Book</CODE> class gains two methods:<P>
<PRE>
  toJSON() {
    return { format: 'Contacts', version: 1, contacts: this.all() };
  }
  static fromJSON(data) {
    if (data?.format !== 'Contacts') {
      throw new Error('This is not a Contacts file');
    }
    const book = new Book();
    for (const c of data.contacts ?? []) book.add(c);
    return book;
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>toJSON</CODE> is a name <CODE>JSON.stringify</CODE> knows: when it meets an object with a
<CODE>toJSON</CODE> method, it turns into text whatever that method returns instead of the object itself. A
<CODE>Book</CODE> holds its contacts in a <CODE>Map</CODE>, which JSON can't show, so <CODE>toJSON</CODE>
gives back a plain object holding an array of them.<P>
<CODE>fromJSON</CODE> goes the other way. The word <CODE>static</CODE> in front of it makes it belong to the
class itself rather than to each book: you call it as <CODE>Book.fromJSON(data)</CODE>, before there is a
book, and it makes one.<P>
The object saved has two fields besides the contacts:<P>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>format: 'Contacts'</CODE> says what the file is, so <CODE>fromJSON</CODE> can refuse a file that
isn't an address book (someone could set any file's type to Contacts) with a clear message rather than
something baffling later.<P>
<LI><CODE>version: 1</CODE> says which version of the format it is. If a later Contacts saves more - a
photograph, say - it can write version 2, and still read version 1 files by looking at this number first.
It costs nothing to put in now, and it's very hard to add later, when there are files without it.<P>
</UL>
Here is the start of a saved address book (<CODE>JSON.stringify(book, null, 1)</CODE> puts each value on a
line of its own, so the file is easy to read):<P>
<PRE>
{
 "format": "Contacts",
 "version": 1,
 "contacts": [
  {
   "first": "Ann",
   "last": "Smith",
</PRE>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Documents</H2>
With several address books open, each needs its own <CODE>Book</CODE>, its own window, the name of its
file, and a note of whether it has changed since it was saved. A class called <CODE>Document</CODE> keeps
those together - one <CODE>Document</CODE> for each open file:<P>
<PRE>
export class Document {
  constructor(app, book = new Book(), filename = '') {
    this.app = app;
    this.book = book;
    this.filename = filename;          // '' until it's saved
    this.modified = false;
    this.view = new MainWindow(this);
    book.on('change', () =&gt; this.setModified(true));
    app.docs.add(this);
  }

  static async load(app, path) {
    const text = await vfs.readText(path);
    const book = Book.fromJSON(JSON.parse(text));
    return new Document(app, book, path);
  }
</PRE>
The constructor makes the document's window (<CODE>MainWindow</CODE> now takes the document rather than the
book, and reaches the book as <CODE>doc.book</CODE>), and listens to the book: any change at all marks the
document as modified. <CODE>app.docs</CODE> is a <CODE>Set</CODE> of the open documents (a Set, as in
chapter 1's Emitter, holds each thing once).<P>
<CODE>load</CODE> is another <CODE>static</CODE> method: it reads a file, turns it back into a
<CODE>Book</CODE> and makes a <CODE>Document</CODE> for it. It is <CODE>async</CODE>, because reading a
file is.<P>

<H3>What else changes in MainWindow</H3>
MainWindow now belongs to a document: its constructor takes the <CODE>Document</CODE> and reaches the book as
<CODE>this.doc.book</CODE> and the rest of the application as <CODE>this.app</CODE>. Besides that:<P>
<UL>
<LI>Each new window opens a little lower and further right than the last
(<CODE>this.app.docs.size % 6</CODE> steps of 24 pixels, starting again after six), so several books don't
hide each other exactly.<P>
<LI>The toolbar gains a Save button, after a <CODE>{ gap: true }</CODE> that leaves a space between the
editing buttons and it; F3 does the same.<P>
<LI>The menu is split into submenus, as a larger application's should be: <I>File</I> (with Save) and
<I>Edit</I> (the entries that were at the top level), then Sort and Find as before.<P>
<LI>Closing the window no longer quits the program: several books can be open, and the program stays on the
icon bar (see Closing, below).<P>
</UL>
The Sample module has gone - the contacts now come from files - and Check gains a test that a book saved as
JSON and loaded again has the same contacts.<P>

<H3>The title bar</H3>
<PRE>
  /** The window's title: the file's name, and * if it's changed. */
  get title() {
    const name = this.filename || '&lt;untitled&gt;';
    return this.modified ? name + ' *' : name;
  }
  setModified(m) {
    this.modified = m;
    this.view.updateTitle();
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------ saving
  get data() { return JSON.stringify(this.book, null, 1) + '\n'; }
  save(path) {
    vfs.writeFile(path, this.data, { filetype: this.app.TYPE });
    this.filename = vfs.canonical(path);
    this.setModified(false);
  }
</PRE>
The RISC OS Style Guide asks for a document's window to show its file's full name, and a <CODE>*</CODE> after
it from the moment it is changed until it is saved - so you can always see what would be lost.
<CODE>get title()</CODE> is a <I>getter</I>: <CODE>doc.title</CODE> looks like a property, but reading it
runs this function, so it is always up to date. <CODE>setModified</CODE> asks the window to show the new
title (<CODE>updateTitle</CODE> in <CODE>MainWindow</CODE> is one line: <CODE>this.win.setTitle(this.doc.title)</CODE>).<P>
<CODE>save</CODE> writes the file with type &amp;1C4, remembers its full name, and clears the
<CODE>*</CODE>. <CODE>JSON.stringify(this.book, null, 1)</CODE> calls the book's <CODE>toJSON</CODE> as
described above.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Saving</H2>
<PRE>
  /** The Save box (as a menu's submenu, or opened by F3). */
  saveBox(then) {
    return saveAs({
      task: this.app.task, filetype: this.app.TYPE,
      filename: this.filename || 'Contacts',
      getData: async () =&gt; this.data,
      save: async (path) =&gt; this.save(path),
      onSaved: (path) =&gt; { if (path) then?.(); },
    });
  }
  /** Save straight away if it has a file, else show the Save box. */
  quickSave(then) {
    if (this.filename) { this.save(this.filename); then?.(); return; }
    const box = this.saveBox(then);
    wimp.menus.open(box, 200, 200, { task: this.app.task });
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>saveBox</CODE> makes the desktop's standard Save box, which you used in the first book: drag its icon to a
directory display, or type a full name and click OK. The window's menu has <I>File &gt; Save</I>, with the
Save box as its submenu, as in every RISC OS application.<P>
<CODE>quickSave</CODE> is what F3 and the Save button do: a document that already has a file is saved to it
straight away; a new one opens the Save box. Both take an optional function, <CODE>then</CODE>, to call once
the document has been saved - you'll see why in the next section.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C7Save" WIDTH=1024 HEIGHT=768></CENTER>
Here Friends is open, and a new, empty address book beside it; F3 in the new one has opened the Save box.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Closing</H2>
When you close a window whose document has changes that haven't been saved, the Style Guide's rule is to ask,
with three buttons: <I>Discard</I> (close it anyway), <I>Cancel</I> (don't close it) and <I>Save</I>.
<CODE>MainWindow</CODE> stops the window closing by itself, and asks the document instead:<P>
<PRE>
    w.on('close', (ev) =&gt; { ev.preventDefault(); this.doc.close(); });
</PRE>
<CODE>ev.preventDefault()</CODE> stops the Wimp's usual action - closing the window - so the document can
decide:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ closing
  /** Close, asking about unsaved changes. Resolves true if closed. */
  async close() {
    if (this.modified) {
      const answer = await askClose(this.app.task);
      if (!answer) return false;                        // Cancel
      if (answer === 'Save') {
        this.quickSave(() =&gt; this.dispose());
        return false;
      }
    }
    this.dispose();
    return true;
  }
  dispose() {
    this.view.dispose();
    this.app.docs.delete(this);
  }
}

// the Style Guide's box: Discard, Cancel, Save
let closeForm = null;
async function askClose(task) {
  closeForm ??= new Form(task, {
    title: 'Contacts', fields: [],
    message: 'This file has been modified.',
    buttons: ['Discard', 'Cancel', 'Save'],
  });
  const r = await closeForm.run();
  return r?.button ?? null;
}
</PRE>
The box is a <CODE>Form</CODE> from chapter 3 with no fields, a message and three buttons. The last button,
Save, is the default, so Return saves; Escape (or Cancel) gives <CODE>null</CODE>. If you choose Save, the
document is saved with <CODE>quickSave</CODE> and closed afterwards, by the function passed as
<CODE>then</CODE> - after the Save box, if it hasn't a file yet. If that Save box is abandoned, the document
stays open, which is just what you'd want.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C7Close" WIDTH=629 HEIGHT=409></CENTER>
<CODE>dispose</CODE> tidies up: it deletes the window (and stops it listening to the book) and takes the
document out of <CODE>app.docs</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>One Contacts, several books</H2>
Because there can now be several address books, there should be only one Contacts: double-clicking a second
file should open it in the Contacts already running, not start another copy. The start of
<CODE>!RunImage</CODE> sees to that:<P>
<PRE>
export default async function start(task, ctx) {
  const file = ctx.argv[0];
  // only one Contacts at a time: pass the file to the one running
  const other = wimp.tasks.find((t) =&gt; t !== task &amp;&amp; t.alive &amp;&amp;
    t.name === task.name);
  if (other) {
    if (file) {
      wimp.sendMessage('DataOpen', { path: file, filetype: TYPE },
        { to: other });
    }
    task.quit();
    return;
  }
</PRE>
A program started with a file - by a double-click, or by dragging it to the application - finds the file's
name in <CODE>ctx.argv[0]</CODE>, the first word of what <CODE>!Run</CODE> passed on. (Don't confuse it with
<CODE>ctx.file</CODE>, which is the full name of the program itself.)<P>
<CODE>wimp.tasks</CODE> is the list of every task running on the desktop. If one of them is another
Contacts, this copy sends it the file in a <CODE>DataOpen</CODE> message - the message the Filer itself
sends when a file is double-clicked - and quits at once.<P>
The rest of <CODE>start</CODE> sets up the application: an object <CODE>app</CODE> holding what every
document shares, including <CODE>open</CODE>, which brings a file's window to the front if it is open
already, and loads it otherwise:<P>
<PRE>
  const app = {
    task, TYPE,
    docs: new Set(),                 // the open address books
    prefs: { sort: 'last' },         // the order of the lists
    area: sprites.spritesFromFile(await vfs.readFile(ctx.dir +
      '.Sprites')),                  // the toolbar's pictures
    /** Open a file (or bring its window to the front). */
    async open(path) {
      const name = vfs.canonical(path);
      const open = [...app.docs].find((d) =&gt; d.filename === name);
      if (open) {
        open.view.win.open({ behind: 'top' });
        return open;
      }
      try {
        return await Document.load(app, name);
      } catch (e) {
        reportError(`Can't load ${vfs.leaf(name)}: ${e.message}`);
        return null;
      }
    },
    refreshAll() { for (const d of app.docs) d.view.refresh(); },
    get modified() { return [...app.docs].some((d) =&gt; d.modified); },
  };
</PRE>
Contacts now has an icon on the icon bar, as applications that edit documents do. Select on it makes a new,
empty address book; dropping a Contacts file on it opens the file. Its menu has just Quit for now:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the icon bar
  const quit = async () =&gt; {
    if (app.modified &amp;&amp; !(await discardChanges(task,
      'Some address books have changes. Quit anyway?'))) return;
    task.quit();
  };
  const menu = () =&gt; new Menu('Contacts', [
    { text: 'Quit', action: quit },
  ]);
  task.addIconbarIcon({
    sprite: '!contacts7', menu,
    onClick: () =&gt; new Document(app),
    onDataLoad: async (ev) =&gt; {
      for (const f of ev.files) {
        if (f.filetype === TYPE) await app.open(f.path);
      }
    },
  });
</PRE>
<CODE>quit</CODE> asks before quitting if any book has changes that haven't been saved.
<CODE>discardChanges(task, message)</CODE> is the desktop's standard question for that, with Discard and Cancel
buttons: it resolves <CODE>true</CODE> if Discard is chosen. <CODE>app.modified</CODE> is a getter that
asks each open document.<P>
Finally, <CODE>DataOpen</CODE>. When you double-click a file, the Filer first sends a <CODE>DataOpen</CODE>
message to every task, in case one of them is already running and would like to open it. Only if no task
claims it does it run the file type's <CODE>Alias$@RunType</CODE> command. Contacts claims Contacts files, by
returning <CODE>true</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ messages
  // a Contacts file double-clicked while we're running
  task.onMessage('DataOpen', (msg) =&gt; {
    if (msg.filetype !== TYPE) return false;
    app.open(msg.path);
    return true;
  });
  task.onMessage('Quit', () =&gt; task.quit());

  if (file) await app.open(file);
</PRE>
The last line opens the file the program was started with, if there was one.<P>

